// Runtime/RuntimeUsage.maxon — WHICH compiler-emitted runtime functions a program references, and what
// each of those in turn needs. ⭐ **ONE walk of the Maxon module, one bit per entry point, and the
// closures that declare what a SYNTHESIZED body calls that a walk of the Maxon module cannot see.**
//
// ⚠⚠ **THIS FILE IS A RELOCATION, NOT A NEW MECHANISM (W49 wave 9), AND THE RELOCATION IS THE POINT.**
// Every line below lived in `Runtime/StringRuntime.maxon` — 760 of that file's 2,229 — where it decided
// the install of the green-thread scheduler, the IOCP substrate, the streaming subprocess API, the array,
// set, map and list runtimes, the descriptor and existential drops, the incref/retain helpers, the union
// destructors and struct cloners, the wall clock, the UCD tables, the grapheme family, managed
// files, managed directories, the command line, standard input and process introspection.
//
// **A String is one tenant of that list and was never its landlord.** The file's name said otherwise, and
// a name that lies is the cheapest kind of wrong fact to leave lying around: it tells the next reader that
// `usesIocp` is a String concern and that a new runtime family's bit belongs in a String file. The two
// String-specific arms (`recordStringMethodUsage`/`closeStringMethodNeeds`) stay here with their siblings
// rather than going back, because what makes them work is that every family follows ONE discipline —
// declared here, checked against each other here, and ORDERED against each other here.
//
// ⚠ **NOTHING ABOUT THE BEHAVIOUR MOVED WITH IT.** No bit was added, removed, renamed or reordered, and
// no closure's arms changed. The shv2 suite reads the same 4,789 and the golden drift the same 1,323
// fragments, by NAME, across the move.
//
// The three rules the whole file rests on, stated once here rather than re-argued per family:
//
//   1. **ONE BIT PER ENTRY POINT.** Vocabulary does not ship ahead of its consumer: a program that only
//      prints must not carry the stderr writer, and one that only reads a String's bytes must not carry
//      the segmenter. Where two near-identical graphs exist, they get two bits.
//   2. **A SYNTHESIZED BODY'S CALLS ARE *DECLARED*, NEVER DISCOVERED.** This scan walks the MAXON module;
//      a runtime function's body is a `StdOp` graph that no Maxon module contains. So `__str_decref`'s
//      `__mm_free` and `__managed_create`'s `__mm_alloc` are stated in the
//      `close*Needs` closures. What a CORPUS body calls is the opposite case — an ordinary Maxon call
//      edge the reachability walk FINDS — and a declared list of those could only go stale.
//   3. **A CLOSURE RUNS AFTER EVERY RECORD, NOT INSIDE THE ARM THAT SET THE BIT**, so the answer cannot
//      depend on the order in which a module happens to NAME its entry points. The order the four
//      `record*Usage` calls run in IS load-bearing and is a rule about FAMILIES: a family that closes
//      into another must record first.

// --- Which compiler-internal runtime functions a module references ---
//
// ⭐ ONE WALK, THREE ANSWERS. `moduleUsesHeap`'s job (does this program touch the heap?) and the two
// String questions (does it print? does it compare strings?) are the SAME traversal — every call op,
// once — so they are answered together rather than by three near-identical walks. Each answer keys a
// DIFFERENT installation, and the heap answer stays `__mm_`-specific: a print-only program must carry
// NO allocator and NO leak check, so `usesHeap` reads `isRuntimeCallee` (the `__mm_` prefix), never
// "any compiler-internal callee".
//
// It asks the MAXON module because the answers are needed BEFORE lowering — the heap decision drives
// `.data` layout, and all three drive which functions get installed into the Std module.

// The set of `__destruct_<U>` symbols a module CALLS. A SET, not a list, because both
// `noteUnionDestructorUsage` and
// `installUnionDestructors` walk EVERY union (`project.enumTypes`) and ask "is this union's destructor
// referenced?" — a per-union membership test. Against a per-drop-site list that is an O(unions × drop
// sites) scan (the classic linear-lookup-in-a-linear-walk quadratic); against a set it is O(1) each, so
// the whole check is O(unions + drop sites). Insert also DEDUPLICATES — a union dropped D times records
// its one symbol once, not D copies.
export typealias DestructorSet = Set with ByteArray

// The set of `__clone_<T>` symbols a module references (P1.7 slice 3b-vi-b) — the CLONE twin of
// `DestructorSet`, collected from the `funcAddr(__clone_<T>)` element-cloner stamps and closed over nesting by
// `noteClonerUsage`, so `installStructCloners`/`installUnionCloners`/`installGenericInstanceCloners`
// synthesize only the referenced cloners. The SAME `Set with ByteArray` type is reused by `typeSupportsDeepClone` as its VISITED-name set (a
// cycle-breaker over struct/union names), since both are membership sets of `ByteArray` names.
export typealias ClonerSet = Set with ByteArray

// The set of `__enum_from_raw_<Enum>` symbols a module references — the `DestructorSet` shape a third time,
// for the third installer that walks EVERY declared enum and asks "is this one's helper referenced?"
// (`installEnumLookupHelpers`). It needs no closure pass: the helper's body compares constants and calls
// nothing, so a referenced helper reaches nothing further.
export typealias EnumLookupSet = Set with ByteArray

// The set a LAYOUT WALK tests membership in, whatever per-type runtime entry it is installing — the role
// `DestructorSet` and `ClonerSet` share, spelled once because `MmRuntime.installStructRuntimeEntries` walks
// ONE roster of struct layouts for both of them (review of W180). They are the same `Set with ByteArray`, so
// the shared walk could have borrowed either name; borrowing one would have made the roster read as the drop
// side's walk that the clone side happens to reuse, which is the reading that let the two drift apart in the
// first place.
export typealias ReferencedEntrySymbols = Set with ByteArray

export type RuntimeUsage
	export var usesHeap as bool
	// The program spawns a green thread (`async`/`await`). Drives `installGtRuntime` + the GT `.data`
	// globals + the two hand-assembled backend chunks. Set alongside `usesHeap` (spawn `__slab_alloc`s the
	// GT struct + arg buffer), from the `asyncCall`/`await` Maxon ops rather than a callee name — the async
	// ops are their own op kind, and their `__gt_*` calls do not exist until lowering.
	export var usesGt as bool
	// The program issues a YIELDING overlapped pipe read (`__gt_io_read`, the `spawnReadLine` probe, P1.5 slice
	// 1a-ii). A SUPERSET of `usesGt` (a read yields the green thread, so it needs the whole scheduler), plus the
	// IOCP substrate: `installIoRuntime` (`__io_init` + `__gt_io_read`), the hand-assembled `__io_completion_loop`
	// chunk, the six `__io_*` `.data` slots, and the run-queue CRITICAL_SECTION that guards the completion thread's
	// cross-thread `__gt_enqueue`. Set by `recordCallUsage` on a `__gt_io_read` call (which also sets `usesGt`).
	export var usesIocp as bool
	// The STREAMING subprocess API (P1.5 dogfood slice 1b) — the handle table + `__gt_subp_*` functions. A SUPERSET
	// of `usesIocp` (the yielding line read rides the completion thread), set by `recordCallUsage` on any
	// `__gt_subp_*` call. Drives `installSubpRuntime` + the `__subp_table` `.data` slot.
	export var usesStreaming as bool
	// W35: the two stream writers, each with its OWN bit under this file's one-bit-per-entry-point
	// discipline — a program that only prints must not carry the stderr writer, and neither declares any
	// transitive floor (see `StreamWriteStdoutName`).
	export var usesWriteStdout as bool
	export var usesWriteStderr as bool
	export var usesStrEq as bool
	export var usesStrCopy as bool
	export var usesIntToString as bool
	// The UNSIGNED decimal renderer (X11). A bit of its own rather than a widening of the one above,
	// because a program that only prints a declared non-negative value references only `__uint_to_string`
	// and must not carry the signed entry point it never calls — the "vocabulary does not ship ahead of
	// its consumer" rule `installStringRuntime` states, applied to a second entry point of one builder.
	export var usesUIntToString as bool
	export var usesBoolToString as bool
	export var usesStrDecref as bool
	// The program touches the `__ManagedMemory` element buffer — as an `Array`, a `Vector`, or the buffer
	// itself (P1.7 slice 1). Any `__managed_*` runtime call turns this on — and, like the String helpers,
	// turns on `usesHeap` too, because those helpers `__mm_alloc`/`__mm_free` the backing record and its
	// element buffer inside their Std bodies, which this Maxon-module scan cannot see. It drives
	// `installManagedMemoryRuntime` (the whole buffer runtime, appended already-lowered).
	// ⚠ Its test is the `__managed_` PREFIX, which is why that prefix may not be `__mm_`: the allocator's own
	// prefix test (`MmRuntime.isRuntimeCallee`) decides `usesHeap`, and one spelling would make every
	// heap-touching program carry this runtime too. See `ManagedMemoryRuntime`'s header.
	export var usesManagedMemory as bool
	// ⚠ **`usesSet` AND `usesMap` STOOD HERE AND WENT WITH THE RUNTIMES THEY DROVE (W105).** Each turned on
	// its container's whole install and `usesHeap` beside it; both installs are deleted, so the bits had no
	// consumer left. Their one surviving reader was the `CharacterSet` family's closure, which stated the
	// `usesHeap` half in its own arms rather than borrowing theirs — and that family went at W129 too, so
	// nothing is left holding a bit on either's behalf.
	// The program touches a `List`. Any `__list_*` call turns this on — and, like `usesManagedMemory`, turns on
	// `usesHeap` too, because the list helpers `__mm_alloc` the chain header and every node and `__mm_free`
	// them again inside their Std bodies, which this Maxon-module scan cannot see. It drives
	// `installListRuntime`. Its OWN bit so a list-free program stays golden-neutral (installs nothing).
	export var usesList as bool
	// The program DROPS a single opaque type-parameter value (`__drop_type_param`, P1.7 slice 3b-v /
	// Finding A) — a consumed feed binding left live at a scope exit, an opaque array element moved out
	// (`.pop()`), OR a bare opaque `T` field reassignment (`self.saved = next`). Its OWN bit, decoupled from
	// `usesManagedMemory`: a bare-field reassign in an ARRAY-FREE generic struct emits `__drop_type_param` yet touches
	// no `__managed_*`, so gating its install on `usesManagedMemory` would leave the call unlinked. `__drop_type_param` is
	// `__`-prefixed but NOT `__mm_`/`__managed_`-prefixed, so a call to it sets no other usage bit; its body only
	// reads the descriptor's `destroyFunc@40` and `callIndirect`s it (no direct alloc/free), so this bit alone
	// drives `installDropTypeParamRuntime` and pulls in no transitive floor of its own.
	export var usesDropTypeParam as bool
	// The program takes a REFERENCE to a single opaque type-parameter value (`__retain_type_param`,
	// W41-opaque) — a shared generic body storing a borrowed `T` into a record it builds. Its OWN bit rather
	// than `usesDropTypeParam`'s, for the reason `usesRetainExistential` is not `usesDropExistential`'s: a body
	// that only stores never drops and a body that only drops never stores, so neither implies the other. Its
	// body reads the descriptor's `retainFunc@64` and `callIndirect`s it, so it pulls in no transitive floor.
	export var usesRetainTypeParam as bool
	// The program DROPS a value held at its INTERFACE type (`__drop_existential`, P1.7a-existentials slice
	// 2) — an owned interface-typed field released by its struct's destructor cascade, or a consumed
	// existential parameter left live at a scope exit. Its OWN bit for `usesDropTypeParam`'s exact reason,
	// with the roles reversed: an interface field drops in a program that instantiates no generic, so gating
	// its install on the descriptor drop's bit would leave THAT call unlinked. Its body only reads the
	// witness table's `destroyFunc@8` and `callIndirect`s it, so it pulls in no transitive floor of its own.
	export var usesDropExistential as bool
	// The program CO-OWNS a value held at its INTERFACE type (`__retain_existential`, P1.7a-existentials
	// slice 2) — a borrowed existential bound into a `var`, or one forwarded to a consuming position. Its
	// OWN bit rather than `usesDropExistential`'s, for the reason `usesRetain` is not `usesIncref`'s: the two
	// have independent callers, so folding either into the other installs an unreachable copy of the unused
	// one. It DOES imply `__mm_incref`, which its body calls — declared through `recordCallUsage`.
	export var usesRetainExistential as bool
	// The program takes a SECOND owner of a heap box (`__mm_incref`). Two producers: the managed-memory runtime's
	// zero-copy slice (a view increfs the parent's element buffer — implied by `usesManagedMemory`, since the call
	// lives in a Std body this scan cannot see), and the PARSER's `try … otherwise` reconciliation, which
	// increfs an ok-edge borrow or an aliased error-edge binding so both merge into one owned phi — a `call
	// __mm_incref` this scan DOES see. Drives `installMmRuntime` to build `__mm_incref`; a program that never
	// increfs (the common one) keeps it out. Set alongside `usesHeap` (an incref is on a `__mm_alloc`'d box).
	export var usesIncref as bool
	// ⭐ **`__mm_is_null` — the `__ManagedMemory` NULL predicate** (`__Builtins.managedIsNull`). Its own bit,
	// for `usesRetain`'s reason one family over: the one call site in the corpus is
	// `stdlib/Subprocess.maxon`'s executable lookup, and a program that never asks the question must not carry
	// the answer. The parser emits the call directly, so this scan sees it.
	export var usesMmIsNull as bool
	// The program co-owns a BORROWED managed-aggregate parameter into a `var` (`var q = p`, OPEN #40),
	// which the parser makes a second owner of the caller's box via `__mm_retain`. Its OWN bit, not
	// `usesIncref`: `__mm_retain` (returns the pointer) and `__mm_incref` (void) have independent callers,
	// so folding either into the other installs a dead function in the program that uses only the other
	// (see `installMmRuntime`). A `call __mm_retain` this scan sees directly. Drives `installMmRuntime` to
	// build `__mm_retain`; a program that never co-owns a borrowed aggregate keeps it out. Set alongside
	// `usesHeap` (a retain is on a `__mm_alloc`'d box).
	export var usesRetain as bool
	// ⭐ The program declares at least one MANAGED top-level `var` (P1.7 slice 2a), so it carries
	// `__module_init` + `__maxon_global_cleanup` and every entry stub calls them around `main`. Set NOT by
	// this Maxon-module scan but by `installManagedGlobalInit`, which is where the decision is made — the
	// same discipline the leak/GT gates follow (see `BackendDispatch.buildBackend`'s header): an entry stub
	// asking the module "is `__module_init` still there?" would be asking a question dead-function
	// elimination can change the answer to, where "was it installed?" is fixed. The RUNTIME floor those two
	// functions need (`__mm_alloc`, `__str_clone`, `__str_decref`, `__managed_decref`) rides the ordinary bits:
	// they are appended to the Maxon module BEFORE `scanRuntimeUsage` walks it, so their calls are seen
	// exactly as user code's are.
	export var usesManagedGlobals as bool
	// The `__destruct_<U>` symbols this module CALLS (P1.3 slice 2) — collected from the drop sites so
	// `installUnionDestructors` synthesizes only the referenced ones, and `noteUnionDestructorUsage`
	// can classify their transitive `__str_decref`/`__mm_free` needs. Full symbols (not names): the
	// consumers query them against `destructorSymbol(name)`, avoiding a byte-substring extract. A
	// SET so that per-union query is O(1) — see `DestructorSet`.
	export var neededDestructors as DestructorSet
	// The program DEEP-CLONES a String (`__str_clone`, P1.7 slice 3b-vi-b) — a managed array/struct copy that
	// clones a String element/field. Its OWN bit, like `usesStrDecref`: `__str_clone` calls `__mm_alloc`/
	// `__str_copy` inside its Std body this scan cannot see, so a reference turns on `usesHeap`/`usesStrCopy`
	// too (`recordCallUsage`). Drives `installStringRuntime` to build `__str_clone`.
	export var usesStrClone as bool
	// The program CO-OWNS a borrowed byte record whose provenance only the RECORD knows (`__str_retain`,
	// W56). Its own bit for the same reason `usesStrClone` has one: its Std body calls `__str_clone` and
	// `__mm_incref`, neither of which this Maxon-module scan can see, so a reference turns on
	// `usesStrClone`/`usesStrCopy`/`usesIncref`/`usesHeap` too (`recordCallUsage`).
	export var usesStrRetain as bool
	// ⚠ **THE P1.8 SLICE C BYTE/ASCII FAMILY'S BITS ARE ALL GONE — ALL EIGHT OF THEM.**
	// `usesStrStartsWith`/`usesStrEndsWith` in W49 wave 1, `usesStrMapCase`/`usesStrReplace`/
	// `usesStrReplaceFirst` in wave 2, `usesStrContains` in wave 4, and `usesStrSplit` with
	// `usesStrFind`/`usesStrRangeEq` in wave 5. All seven members are the CORPUS's now (struck from
	// `Parser.stringSurfaceMemberNames`), so nothing names their entry points and a bit for any of them could
	// only ever read false. The corpus bodies reach `byteAt`, `setByte`, `addressableBytes` and
	// `__ManagedMemory.slice`, whose own doors are still shv2's and keep their own bits below.
	//
	// ⚠ **THE LAST TWO ARE THE ONES THAT CAME OFF DIFFERENTLY, AND THE ORDER IS THE WHOLE OF IT.**
	// `usesStrFind`/`usesStrRangeEq` were never set by a member — they were set TRANSITIVELY, by
	// `closeStringMethodNeeds`, from whichever member reached the search underneath. With `contains` (wave 4)
	// and the `StringIndex` searches (wave 3) already gone, `usesStrSplit` was the last thing that could turn
	// `usesStrFind` on, and `usesStrFind` the last thing that could turn `usesStrRangeEq` on — so all three
	// die together and none of them could die sooner. See the P1.8 Slice C grave at the head of this file for
	// why an entry point may only be deleted once every PRODUCER of its callee is gone.
	// The P1.8 Slice E entry point that remains, with its OWN bit under this file's one-bit-per-entry-point
	// discipline: a program that builds no String from bytes must not carry the classifying scan.
	export var usesStrFromBytes as bool
	export var usesStrOfBuffer as bool
	export var usesMmToCString as bool
	// The byte-view door, with its OWN bit under this file's one-bit-per-entry-point discipline: a program
	// that never reads a fused wrapper's inline `managed` must not carry the view builder. It declares
	// `usesManagedMemory`/`usesHeap` in `closeStringMethodNeeds` — it calls `__managed_create` and `__mm_incref` inside
	// its synthesized body, which this Maxon-module scan cannot see.
	//
	// ⚠ **`usesStrByteAt` AND `usesStrByteAtOrPanic` SAT BESIDE IT AND WENT WITH THEIR ENTRY POINTS (W49
	// wave 7)** — see the byte-door header at the head of this file. They were the file's example of two bits
	// for two near-identical graphs (so a program reaching only the throwing read did not carry the other's
	// `mrt_panic` call site); with both members retired onto the corpus, neither bit had a writer.
	export var usesStrBytesView as bool
	// The program copies a MANAGED-element array (`__managed_slice_managed`/`__managed_clone_managed`/
	// `__managed_append_managed`, P1.7 slice 3b-vi-b). Its OWN bit, DECOUPLED from `usesManagedMemory`, so a program that
	// only uses TRIVIAL arrays does not carry the three managed-copy helpers. Set alongside `usesManagedMemory`/
	// `usesHeap` (the helpers are `__managed_`-prefixed). Drives the managed-copy install in `installManagedMemoryRuntime`.
	export var usesManagedElementCopy as bool
	// The program copies an OPAQUE-`T` array (`__managed_slice_opaque`/`__managed_append_opaque`, P1.7 slice
	// 3b-vi-b-β) — a `slice`/`append` on an `Array with <type parameter>` field in a generic body. Its OWN bit,
	// DECOUPLED from `usesManagedMemory`, so a program that touches no opaque-array copy does not carry the wrappers.
	// Set alongside `usesManagedElementCopy` (`recordCallUsage`), because each wrapper's managed
	// branch calls the α `__managed_*_managed` helpers. Drives the opaque-copy install in `installManagedMemoryRuntime`.
	export var usesOpaqueElementCopy as bool
	// The `__clone_<T>` symbols this module references (P1.7 slice 3b-vi-b) — the CLONE twin of
	// `neededDestructors`, collected from the `funcAddr(__clone_<T>)` element-cloner stamps and closed over
	// nesting by `noteClonerUsage`, so `installStructCloners`/`installUnionCloners`/
	// `installGenericInstanceCloners` synthesize only the referenced cloners. `usesHeap` follows any cloner
	// (the box it allocates).
	export var neededCloners as ClonerSet
	// The `__enum_from_raw_<Enum>` symbols this module CALLS — the `neededDestructors` shape with no closure
	// pass, so `installEnumLookupHelpers` synthesizes only the referenced helpers. See `EnumLookupSet`.
	export var neededEnumLookups as EnumLookupSet
	// The program reads the WALL clock (`__clock_now_unix_s`, `__Builtins.currentUnixTimeSeconds`). Its OWN
	// bit, DECOUPLED from `usesGt` and from `usesHeap`, and that decoupling is the point: a calendar read is
	// a leaf that calls one Win32 import and divides, with no relationship to the green-thread scheduler
	// whose `__gt_now_ns` answers the OTHER clock question. Folding it under `usesGt` would install the
	// entire scheduler — plus the allocator and the leak gate under it — for a program that asks what
	// today's date is. Drives `installClockRuntime` + the one `__clock_wall_ticks` `.data` slot.
	export var usesWallClock as bool
	// ⭐ **THE P1.8 SLICE B GRAPHEME FAMILY — ONE BIT PER ENTRY POINT**, following this file's existing
	// discipline rather than one `usesGrapheme` blanket: a program that only asks
	// `graphemeBreakProperty(cp)` must carry neither the segmenter nor the UTF-8 decoders, and a program
	// that never touches a String at all must carry none of it. Each is set TRANSITIVELY by
	// `recordCallUsage` from whichever entry point the module actually names (a `__gr_count` reference
	// turns on `__gr_end`, which turns on `__gr_break` and the decoders, which turn on `__gbp` and
	// `__ext_pict`) — the same "declare what the synthesized body calls, because this Maxon-module scan
	// cannot see inside it" rule the String method family already follows.
	// `__char_at` MINTS a Character — one `__mm_alloc`'d fused record per call, blitted with `__str_copy`
	// — so it declares the heap and the copy loop that `emitBuildOwnedInlineString` puts inside its body.
	export var usesCharAt as bool
	export var usesCharFromCodepoint as bool
	// `__char_cmp` reads two BORROWED records' `buffer@0`/`length@8` and walks their bytes, allocating
	// nothing and calling nothing — `__char_ascii`'s floor-free shape, so it has no arm in
	// `closeGraphemeNeeds` either.
	export var usesCharCmp as bool
	// `__char_ascii` reads two fields of a BORROWED record and calls nothing, so it declares no transitive
	// floor at all — hence no arm in `closeGraphemeNeeds`. `usesCharCmp` above is the other such bit.
	export var usesCharAsciiValue as bool
	// `__str_to_bytes` PUSHES into a caller-created `Array with Byte`, so it declares the managed-memory runtime
	// (and the heap under it) in `closeGraphemeNeeds`. It is the LAST entry point that fills an array the
	// parser built — `__str_split` was the other, and retired at W49 wave 5.
	export var usesStrToBytes as bool
	// ⚠ **`usesStrToCodepoints`/`usesStrToUtf16` STOOD HERE AND WENT AT W49 WAVE 6** with the two decoding
	// views, onto the corpus's lazy `CodepointView`/`UTF16View`. They were separate bits under this file's
	// one-bit-per-entry-point discipline — a program asking for the bytes must not carry a UTF-8 decoder, and
	// one asking for the codepoints must not carry the surrogate encoder — and that discipline is why their
	// removal takes nothing else with it: `usesStrToBytes` never stood for any of the three.
	// ⚠ **THE P1.8 SLICE B2 `StringIndex` FAMILY HAS NO BITS LEFT, AND NO FILE EITHER.** Six went with the
	// five members that retired onto `stdlib/String.maxon` at W49 wave 3; the last two
	// (`usesStrIndexAdvance`/`usesStrIndexSlice`) went at wave 4 with `String.slice`, which was the only
	// caller `__strix_advance` and `__strix_slice` had. `Runtime/StringIndexRuntime.maxon` is deleted, not
	// emptied — the corpus bodies behind every one of them are ordinary Maxon call edges now, which the
	// reachability walk FINDS rather than being told.
	// ⭐ **THE UNICODE `General_Category` TABLES (P1.8 Slice D, R2; the two raw loads W64).** Its own bit, and
	// DECOUPLED from every String bit: `__ucd_bmp_at`/`__ucd_supp_at` are LEAVES that read a `.rdata` blob and
	// do integer arithmetic — no heap, no copy loop, no segmenter — so a program that classifies a codepoint
	// carries nothing else, and a program that never does carries neither the 65,536-byte BMP table nor the
	// supplementary one. Set by `recordCallUsage` on any `__ucd_` reference, which is what the corpus
	// `unicodeGeneralCategory` lowers to (`Parser.parseUcdTableLoad`).
	//
	// ⚠ **A THIRD ENTRY POINT, `__ucd_cat`, FED THIS BIT UNTIL W129** — the whole lookup as one synthesized
	// graph, emitted by `__cs_contains` and by nothing else. Both went together; the corpus writes the same
	// algorithm out of the two raw reads.
	export var usesUcdCategory as bool
	// ⚠⚠ **THE P1.8 SLICE D `CharacterSet` FAMILY HELD THREE BITS HERE — `usesCharSetMake`/`Decref`/`Contains`
	// — AND ALL THREE WENT WITH THE `__cs_*` RUNTIME (W129).** `W115` listed `stdlib/CharacterSet.maxon`, after
	// which nothing could emit any of the three callees, so no arm could set any of the three bits. Two facts
	// from that family are worth keeping, because neither is visible from the corpus:
	//
	//   * a fourth bit, `usesStrTrim`, went at W49 wave 4 with the three trims. It was the one bit here that
	//     reached into all THREE sibling families, which is what fixed the recording ORDER `recordCallUsage`
	//     still keeps — that order is a rule about families, not about the arm that first needed it;
	//   * the family's closure was the last reader of `usesSet`/`usesMap` (see those bits' note above), and it
	//     carried the `usesHeap` those two used to declare.
	// R4.1: the program opens a file. Unlike the wall clock it DOES declare a transitive dependency —
	// `__mf_open_read` allocates a NUL-terminated scratch copy of the path and the handle box through
	// `__mm_alloc`/`__mm_free`, so it carries `usesHeap` too (set together below).
	export var usesManagedFile as bool
	// R4.3: the program searches, creates or asks about a DIRECTORY. Its own bit, so a file-only program
	// carries none of the directory runtime. Like the file family it declares a transitive dependency on
	// `usesHeap` (the find block, the box and the path scratch are all `__mm_alloc`'d) — and, unlike it,
	// ALSO on `usesManagedMemory`: `__md_filename`/`__md_current_path` build their `__ManagedMemory` answer through
	// `__managed_create`/`__managed_reserve`, calls that live in a Std body this Maxon-module scan cannot see.
	export var usesManagedDirectory as bool
	// The program reads its COMMAND LINE. Like the directory family it declares a transitive dependency on
	// `usesHeap` (the scratch buffer is a raw alloc) AND on `usesManagedMemory`: `__cl_arg` builds its
	// `__ManagedMemory` answer through `__managed_create`/`__managed_reserve` inside a Std body this Maxon-module
	// scan cannot see.
	export var usesCommandLine as bool
	// W5: the program reads STANDARD INPUT. It carries `usesHeap` (the out word is a raw alloc) AND
	// `usesManagedMemory`: `__con_read_stdin` answers with an `Array` record it builds through
	// `__managed_create`/`__managed_reserve` in a Std body this Maxon-module scan cannot see.
	export var usesConsoleStdin as bool
	// W5: the program asks for its own EXECUTABLE PATH. Its own bit rather than sharing the one above, under
	// this file's one-bit-per-entry-point discipline: the two serve different stdlib modules and reach
	// different OS APIs, so a program that reads stdin must not carry a path walk it never calls. Same two
	// transitive bits, for the same two reasons — the grow-and-retry buffer is a raw alloc, and the answer is
	// an `Array` record built one tier down.
	export var usesProcessIntrospection as bool

	static function create() returns RuntimeUsage
		return Self{usesHeap: false, usesGt: false, usesIocp: false, usesStreaming: false, usesWriteStdout: false, usesWriteStderr: false, usesStrEq: false, usesStrCopy: false, usesIntToString: false, usesUIntToString: false, usesBoolToString: false, usesStrDecref: false, usesManagedMemory: false, usesList: false, usesDropTypeParam: false, usesRetainTypeParam: false, usesDropExistential: false, usesRetainExistential: false, usesIncref: false, usesMmIsNull: false, usesRetain: false, usesManagedGlobals: false, neededDestructors: DestructorSet.create(), usesStrClone: false, usesStrRetain: false, usesStrFromBytes: false, usesStrOfBuffer: false, usesMmToCString: false, usesStrBytesView: false, usesManagedElementCopy: false, usesOpaqueElementCopy: false, neededCloners: ClonerSet.create(), neededEnumLookups: EnumLookupSet.create(), usesWallClock: false, usesCharAt: false, usesCharCmp: false, usesCharFromCodepoint: false, usesCharAsciiValue: false, usesStrToBytes: false, usesUcdCategory: false, usesManagedFile: false, usesManagedDirectory: false, usesCommandLine: false, usesConsoleStdin: false, usesProcessIntrospection: false}
	end 'create'
end 'RuntimeUsage'

// `unreachableStdlib` names the functions this scan must ignore — the STDLIB functions no path from
// `main` reaches (`StdlibFacts.unreachable`, see StdlibSource). Dead-function elimination drops them
// before the back end, so the floor they would ask for is a floor for code the program does not
// contain: a `Clock` nothing calls must not install the green-thread scheduler and the wall-clock
// `.data` slot. It never contains a USER function — an unreached user body still speaks for the
// program — so the answer for every stdlib-free program is unchanged.
export function scanRuntimeUsage(module MaxonModule, unreachableStdlib FunctionNameSet, funcSignatures FuncSignatureMap) returns RuntimeUsage
	var usage = RuntimeUsage.create()
	for func in module.functions 'eachFunc'
		if unreachableStdlib.contains(func.name) 'notInTheProgram'
			continue
		end 'notInTheProgram'
		for blockRef in func.blockRefs 'eachBlock'
			let block = try module.blocks.get(blockRef) otherwise panic("scanRuntimeUsage: blockRef from func.blockRefs not in module.blocks")
			for opIdx in block.opRefs 'eachOp'
				let op = try module.ops.get(opIdx) otherwise panic("scanRuntimeUsage: opIdx {opIdx} not in module.ops")
				classifyRuntimeCall(op, usage: usage, funcSignatures: funcSignatures)
			end 'eachOp'
		end 'eachBlock'
	end 'eachFunc'
	return usage
end 'scanRuntimeUsage'

function classifyRuntimeCall(op MaxonOp, usage RuntimeUsage, funcSignatures FuncSignatureMap)
	match op 'op'
		call(_, callee, _, _, _, _, _, _) then recordCallUsage(callee, usage: usage)
		literal to stringLiteral then break 'op'
		// A throwing call names a user callee whose runtime usage is scanned exactly as `call`'s is; the
		// throw/propagate terminators and `panicAbort` name no user callee (panic exits directly) (P1.4b).
		tryCall(_, _, callee, _, _, _, _, _, _) then recordCallUsage(callee, usage: usage)
		throwError to panicAbort then break 'op'
		// P1.5-A1: an `indirectCall` calls through a code POINTER and names no runtime callee, so it records
		// nothing. A `functionRef` naming a USER function is likewise a bare `lea` of no runtime consequence —
		// but one naming a COMPILER-EMITTED callee is the address of an array element destructor
		// stamped into `element_destroy@40` (P1.7 slice 3), and THAT reference must install its target (a
		// `__str_decref`/`__destruct_<T>`/`__mm_decref`/`__managed_decref`) exactly as a direct `call` to it would —
		// its transitive needs then close through `noteDestructorUsage`. Recorded through the same `recordCallUsage`.
		functionRef(_, funcName) then recordFunctionRefUsage(funcName, usage: usage, funcSignatures: funcSignatures)
		indirectCall then break 'op'
		// P1.5-B1a/B2b: `async`/`await`/`try await` carry the green-thread scheduler. Detected by OP KIND
		// (their `__gt_*` calls do not exist until lowering), and imply the HEAP too — `__gt_spawn`
		// `__slab_alloc`s the GT struct and the argument buffer.
		asyncCall to tryAwait then recordGtUsage(usage)
		// `sizeof(T)` lowers to a plain `loadIndirect` of the layout descriptor — it names no runtime callee
		// and pulls in no runtime floor (the descriptor is pure `.rdata`), so it records no usage (P1.6-B1).
		sizeofType then break 'op'
		constArrayLiteral then recordConstArrayLiteralUsage(usage)
		// The jump table names no runtime callee and pulls in no runtime floor — a bare indexed branch.
		switch then break 'op'
		// A witness dispatch names no runtime callee and pulls in no runtime floor — the fused call reads a
		// `.rdata` witness table and jumps; the dispatched user impl is scanned on its own (P1.7a slice 2a).
		// The throwing form adds an error flag, not a runtime callee.
		witnessDispatch to witnessTryDispatch then break 'op'
		// A panic names no runtime callee under either spelling — it exits directly.
		panicAbortValue then break 'op'
		// The error-flag encode names no runtime callee and pulls in no runtime floor — it lowers to a `const`
		// and an `add`.
		errorFlagEncode then break 'op'
	end 'op'
end 'classifyRuntimeCall'

// An `async`/`await` op turns on both scheduler and heap: the scheduler runs the green threads, and the
// heap floor supplies the `__slab_alloc` that `__gt_spawn` and the async arg-buffer spill need.
function recordGtUsage(usage RuntimeUsage)
	usage.usesGt = true
	usage.usesHeap = true
end 'recordGtUsage'

// A CONSTANT array literal over an immortal `.rdata` blob — a `b"…"` byte string, or a packed
// `[0 as Q, …]` — turns on both the managed-memory runtime and the heap under it, the same pair any `__managed_*` call
// declares (`recordCallUsage`'s array arm), because the literal IS an owned Array record (`__mm_alloc`)
// whose `.count()`/`.get()`/drop go through the managed-memory runtime. Both producers of `constArrayLiteral` need
// exactly this pair, which is why one recorder still serves the op after it stopped being byte-string-only.
function recordConstArrayLiteralUsage(usage RuntimeUsage)
	usage.usesManagedMemory = true
	usage.usesHeap = true
end 'recordConstArrayLiteralUsage'

// A `functionRef` whose target is a COMPILER-EMITTED callee is an array element destructor's address stamped
// into `element_destroy@40` (P1.7 slice 3) — its target must be installed exactly as a direct `call` to it
// would, so it routes through the SAME `recordCallUsage` (turning on the heap/`usesStrDecref`/`usesManagedMemory` bit
// or adding to `neededDestructors`, per the callee). A `functionRef` to a USER function is a plain
// code-pointer `lea` of no runtime consequence and records nothing (the P1.5-A1 behaviour, unchanged).
//
// ⚠ **THE TEST IS `isSignaturelessCompilerCallee` AND NOT THE `__` PREFIX (A1r review)**, because since D6/A1r
// a `__` name may be DECLARED — in `stdlib/Builtins.maxon` — and a value use of THAT is the USER case: it is
// a `funcAddr` of the name's `__fnref_` thunk (`lowerFunctionRef`, which parts the same two ways on the same
// predicate), never an installable runtime entry. Keyed on the prefix, `recordCallUsage` was asked about a
// user function; today's roster maps no such name to a bit, so nothing moved — but the question was already
// the wrong one, and a `Builtins.maxon` declaration sharing a runtime prefix (`__mm_`) would have installed a
// runtime floor for a program that never calls into it.
function recordFunctionRefUsage(funcName ByteArray, usage RuntimeUsage, funcSignatures FuncSignatureMap)
	if isSignaturelessCompilerCallee(funcName, funcSignatures: funcSignatures) 'runtimeAddress'
		recordCallUsage(funcName, usage: usage)
	end 'runtimeAddress'
end 'recordFunctionRefUsage'

// ⭐ **THE ONE CALLEE-NAME → RUNTIME-USAGE-BIT MAP.** Exported so `noteDestructorUsage` can root a
// managed opaque-`T` `Array` element's destructor (P1.7 slice 3b-iii) through the IDENTICAL mapping the
// module scan gives a callee named directly — a `__str_decref` turns on the String runtime, a `__managed_decref`
// the managed-memory runtime, a `__destruct_<T>` joins `neededDestructors` — so the reloc-baked destructor is
// installed for the writer to resolve exactly as if the module had called it.
export function recordCallUsage(callee ByteArray, usage RuntimeUsage)
	if isRuntimeCallee(callee) 'heap'
		usage.usesHeap = true
	end 'heap'
	// A DIRECT `__gt_*` runtime call carries the whole green-thread scheduler (P1.5 mid-body-yield). `async`/
	// `await`/`try await` are detected by op KIND above because their `__gt_*` calls do not exist until
	// lowering, but `__Builtins.sleep(ms)` lowers in the FRONT END to a `call __gt_sleep`, which IS a Maxon
	// `call` this scan sees — so any `__gt_*` callee here turns on the scheduler (and, via `recordGtUsage`,
	// the heap). A source `sleep(ms)` reaches it one frame down, inside `stdlib/Sleep.maxon`'s body, which
	// this scan walks because stdlib source is merged into the module it walks — but ONLY IF A PATH FROM
	// `main` REACHES THAT BODY. `scanRuntimeUsage`'s `unreachableStdlib` filter (above) skips the rest, and
	// that qualifier is the load-bearing half, not a footnote: it is what keeps listing a stdlib module
	// byte-neutral for a program that does not use it, and it is why an unreached `sleep(ms)` compiles for a
	// target with no `__gt_sleep` while an unreached `__Builtins.sleep(ms)` in USER code is still refused.
	if isGtRuntimeCallee(callee) 'gt'
		recordGtUsage(usage)
	end 'gt'
	// A `__gt_io_read` call (the `spawnReadLine` probe, P1.5 slice 1a-ii) additionally carries the IOCP substrate:
	// the completion port + completion thread + wake event + run-queue lock. `usesGt` is already set by the `__gt_`
	// prefix above; this bit installs the IOCP-only pieces.
	if callee.equals(IoReadName) 'ioread'
		usage.usesIocp = true
	end 'ioread'
	// `__mm_is_null` is a heap PREDICATE, not a subprocess entry — it reads a pointer and nothing else — so it
	// carries only itself. See `usesMmIsNull`.
	if callee.equals(MmIsNullName) 'mmIsNull'
		usage.usesMmIsNull = true
	end 'mmIsNull'
	// A `__gt_subp_*` call (the P1.5 streaming subprocess API, slice 1b) carries the whole streaming runtime: the
	// handle table + the `__gt_subp_*` functions, which ride the IOCP substrate (`usesIocp`) and build owned Strings
	// from raw bytes (`__mm_alloc`/`__str_copy` inside their bodies — `usesHeap`/`usesStrCopy`, declared here because
	// this Maxon-module scan cannot see those transitive calls). `usesGt`/`usesHeap` are already set by the `__gt_`
	// prefix above; these bits install the streaming-only pieces.
	if isSubprocessRuntimeCallee(callee) 'streaming'
		usage.usesStreaming = true
		usage.usesIocp = true
		usage.usesStrCopy = true
		// ⭐ **THE ATTACHED HALF ADDS THREE MORE TRANSITIVE BITS**, declared here for the reason the three
		// above are: this Maxon-module scan sees only the callee the PARSER emitted, never what that entry's
		// hand-built Std body calls one tier down. `__gt_subp_read_out_bytes` and the two result-buffer
		// readers build byte-element `Array` records (`__managed_create`/`__managed_reserve`), the readers
		// drop the String the line reader answers (`__str_decref`), and `__gt_subp_last_error` renders the
		// Win32 error number with `__int_to_string`.
		usage.usesManagedMemory = true
		usage.usesStrDecref = true
		usage.usesIntToString = true
	end 'streaming'
	// A `__clock_*` call carries the WALL clock and NOTHING ELSE — no scheduler, no heap, no leak gate. It is
	// the ONE runtime family here that declares no transitive dependency, and that is a fact about the code
	// rather than an omission: `__clock_now_unix_s`'s Std body reads one `.data` word through one Win32 import
	// and divides, calling nothing this Maxon-module scan cannot see.
	if isClockRuntimeCallee(callee) 'wallClock'
		usage.usesWallClock = true
	end 'wallClock'
	// R4.1: a `__mf_*` call. It carries `usesHeap` with it, unlike the clock above — the open allocates a
	// NUL-terminated scratch copy of the path and the handle box, and frees the scratch.
	//
	// ⭐ **AND IT CARRIES THE WHOLE SCHEDULER**, because filesystem I/O YIELDS: every `__mf_*` that makes a
	// kernel call opens with `__gt_io_park` (see `GtIoParkName`), a `__gt_` call one tier down that this
	// Maxon-module scan cannot see — the same reason the streaming family declares its bits by hand rather
	// than relying on the prefix test above. Declared here, once, for both filesystem families.
	if isManagedFileRuntimeCallee(callee) 'managedFile'
		usage.usesManagedFile = true
		usage.usesHeap = true
		recordGtUsage(usage)
	end 'managedFile'
	// R4.3: a `__md_*` call. It carries `usesHeap` (the find block, the box and the path scratch) AND
	// `usesManagedMemory`, which the file family does not need: `__md_filename`/`__md_current_path` answer with an
	// `Array` record they build through `__managed_create`/`__managed_reserve` inside their Std bodies, and this
	// Maxon-module scan cannot see a call one tier down. Left off, those two would link against nothing.
	if isManagedDirectoryRuntimeCallee(callee) 'managedDirectory'
		usage.usesManagedDirectory = true
		usage.usesHeap = true
		usage.usesManagedMemory = true
		recordGtUsage(usage)
	end 'managedDirectory'
	// A `__cl_*` call. Same two transitive bits as the directory family and for the same reasons: the scratch
	// buffer is a raw alloc, and `__cl_arg` answers with an `Array` record it builds one tier down.
	if isCommandLineRuntimeCallee(callee) 'commandLine'
		usage.usesCommandLine = true
		usage.usesHeap = true
		usage.usesManagedMemory = true
	end 'commandLine'
	// W5: a `__con_*` or a `__proc_*` call. Same two transitive bits as the command-line family and for the
	// same two reasons — a raw-alloc scratch word (or buffer), and an `Array` record each answers with from a
	// Std body this Maxon-module scan cannot see. Left off, both would link against nothing.
	if isConsoleRuntimeCallee(callee) 'consoleStdin'
		usage.usesConsoleStdin = true
		usage.usesHeap = true
		usage.usesManagedMemory = true
	end 'consoleStdin'
	if isProcessRuntimeCallee(callee) 'processIntrospection'
		usage.usesProcessIntrospection = true
		usage.usesHeap = true
		usage.usesManagedMemory = true
	end 'processIntrospection'
	if callee.equals(StreamWriteStdoutName) 'writestdout'
		usage.usesWriteStdout = true
	end 'writestdout'
	if callee.equals(StreamWriteStderrName) 'writestderr'
		usage.usesWriteStderr = true
	end 'writestderr'
	if callee.equals(StrEqName) 'streq'
		usage.usesStrEq = true
	end 'streq'
	if callee.equals(StrCopyName) 'strcopy'
		usage.usesStrCopy = true
	end 'strcopy'
	if callee.equals(IntToStringName) 'inttostr'
		usage.usesIntToString = true
	end 'inttostr'
	if callee.equals(UIntToStringName) 'uinttostr'
		usage.usesUIntToString = true
	end 'uinttostr'
	if callee.equals(BoolToStringName) 'booltostr'
		usage.usesBoolToString = true
	end 'booltostr'
	// `__str_decref` calls `__mm_alloc`/`__mm_free` inside its Std body, which this Maxon-module scan
	// cannot see — so its TRANSITIVE heap dependency is declared here rather than discovered.
	if callee.equals(StrDecrefName) 'strdecref'
		usage.usesStrDecref = true
		usage.usesHeap = true
	end 'strdecref'
	// A `__mm_incref` the PARSER emitted (the `try … otherwise` phi reconciliation) — install the incref
	// helper for it, not only for the managed-memory runtime's `usesManagedMemory`. `usesHeap` follows because the box it
	// increfs was `__mm_alloc`'d (which set `usesHeap` at its own call site anyway).
	if callee.equals(MmIncrefName) 'incref'
		usage.usesIncref = true
		usage.usesHeap = true
	end 'incref'
	// A `__mm_retain` the PARSER emitted (co-owning a borrowed managed-aggregate parameter into a `var`,
	// OPEN #40) — install the retain helper for it. `usesHeap` follows because the box it retains was
	// `__mm_alloc`'d (which set `usesHeap` at that box's own allocation site anyway).
	if callee.equals(MmRetainName) 'retain'
		usage.usesRetain = true
		usage.usesHeap = true
	end 'retain'
	// Any `__managed_*` call carries the whole managed-memory runtime, and the heap under it: `__managed_create`/`__managed_grow`
	// `__mm_alloc` the record and the element buffer, `__managed_decref` `__mm_free`s them — none of which this
	// Maxon-module scan sees, since those calls live in the synthesized Std bodies. So a single reference
	// declares both bits, exactly as `__str_decref` does for its own transitive heap use.
	if isManagedMemoryRuntimeCallee(callee) 'array'
		usage.usesManagedMemory = true
		usage.usesHeap = true
	end 'array'
	// ⚠ **A `__set_*` ARM AND A `__map_*` ARM STOOD HERE UNTIL W105**, each declaring its container's install
	// plus the heap under it. Both runtimes are deleted and no lowering path can mint either prefix, so the
	// arms could only ever have answered false — the shape `isStringIndexRuntimeCallee` left in at W49.
	// Any `__list_*` call carries the whole list runtime, and the heap under it — the `__managed_*` arm above
	// over a CHAIN instead of a buffer: `__list_create` and every insertion `__mm_alloc` (the header, then one
	// node each) and `__list_decref` `__mm_free`s all of them, none of which this Maxon-module scan sees.
	// ⛔⛔ **`usesIncref` RIDES HERE AGAIN (`BATCH37`), AND THE REASON IT WAS REMOVED HAS EXPIRED RATHER
	// THAN BEEN WRONG.** `W138` deleted it correctly: its own justification named a synthesized
	// `__list_node_handle` body that the second ruling had already deleted, and at that tick EVERY node
	// handle's `__mm_incref` was emitted by the PARSER — an ordinary `MaxonOp.call` this very scan walks,
	// declared by the `__mm_incref` arm above. The removal was even sabotage-checked and the suite stayed
	// green, which was a true measurement of the tree it was taken on.
	//
	// ⛔ **`BATCH37` MADE IT FALSE AGAIN: THE FOUR THROWING NAVIGATORS INCREF INSIDE THEIR OWN Std BODIES.**
	// `__list_head_checked` and its three siblings take the handle's `+1` on their ok path because the parser
	// structurally cannot (see `Parser.emitManagedListNodeHandle`), and `__list_reinsert_*` increfs a DETACHED
	// node it adopts — three `__mm_incref` call sites this Maxon-module scan cannot see, exactly like the
	// `__mm_alloc`s the line below it declares. MEASURED as a compiler PANIC, not a miscompile:
	// `resolveCallFixups: call to unknown function '__mm_incref'` on `/specs/managed-list.md:core.head-empty-throws`,
	// a program whose only list operations are `create` and `head`.
	//
	// ⇒ **A DELETED PRODUCER IS ONLY DEAD FOR AS LONG AS ITS PREMISE HOLDS**, and the premise here — *"every
	// list-family incref is visible to this scan"* — is a property of the RUNTIME BODIES, which is why it is
	// restated beside them rather than left as an inference off the parser's emit.
	if isListRuntimeCallee(callee) 'list'
		usage.usesList = true
		usage.usesHeap = true
		usage.usesIncref = true
	end 'list'
	// The single-value descriptor-gated drop (P1.7 slice 3b-v / Finding A). Decoupled from `usesManagedMemory` so an
	// array-free bare-field reassign links: it is `__`-prefixed but neither `__mm_`- nor `__managed_`-prefixed, so
	// none of the arms above sees it. Its body only reads the descriptor and `callIndirect`s the destructor
	// (no direct `__mm_alloc`/`__mm_free`), so it declares no other bit — see `usesDropTypeParam`.
	if callee.equals(DropTypeParamName) 'droptypeparam'
		usage.usesDropTypeParam = true
	end 'droptypeparam'
	// The single-value descriptor-gated RETAIN (W41-opaque) — the drop above's inverse, and its `usesHeap`
	// rides for `__retain_existential`'s stated reason: this entry names no callee of its own (it
	// `callIndirect`s `retainFunc@64`), and every word that slot can hold is a heap callee
	// (`__str_clone`/`__mm_retain`), so a program that emits this entry may need the allocator whether or not
	// its own source allocates. The relocation scan runs after `globalData.layOut` and cannot supply the bit.
	if callee.equals(RetainTypeParamName) 'retaintypeparam'
		usage.usesRetainTypeParam = true
		usage.usesHeap = true
	end 'retaintypeparam'
	// The existential's drop (P1.7a-existentials slice 2) — the same shape as the line above and for the same
	// reasons: `__`-prefixed but neither `__mm_`- nor `__managed_`-prefixed, so no arm above sees it, and its body
	// only reads the witness table's `destroyFunc@8` and `callIndirect`s it, so it declares no other bit.
	// ⚠ It does NOT set `usesHeap`: a struct DESTRUCTOR's cascade reaches it from a Std body this scan cannot
	// see, and that destructor's own `__mm_free` already declares the heap (`noteDestructorUsage`).
	if callee.equals(DropExistentialName) 'dropexistential'
		usage.usesDropExistential = true
	end 'dropexistential'
	// The existential's CO-OWNERSHIP (P1.7a-existentials slice 2). Its body names NO callee — since slice 2b it
	// `callIndirect`s the witness's `retainFunc@16`, exactly as its drop twin `callIndirect`s `destroyFunc@8` —
	// so what it reaches is declared by the RELOCATIONS that fill those words (`noteRelocatedFunctionUsage`),
	// not here. It used to declare `usesIncref` for a hardcoded `__mm_incref` that no longer exists.
	// ⚠ **`usesHeap` STILL RIDES, AND IT IS THE ONE BIT THE RELOCATION SCAN CANNOT SUPPLY**: that scan runs
	// after `globalData.layOut`, so a program whose heap is first declared there PANICS
	// (`noteRelocatedFunctionUsage`'s `heapArrivedTooLate` gate) with a `.data` image short by the allocator's
	// globals. Every non-zero ownership word names a heap callee (`__str_clone`/`__str_decref`/`__mm_retain`/a
	// `__destruct_<T>`), so a program that emits this entry may need the allocator whether or not its own
	// source ever allocates — `widen("abc")` is exactly that program, and it is why the bit is taken here
	// rather than inferred later.
	if callee.equals(RetainExistentialName) 'retainexistential'
		usage.usesRetainExistential = true
		usage.usesHeap = true
	end 'retainexistential'
	// A managed-payload union's scope-exit drop (`__destruct_<U>`). It frees the box (`__mm_free` — heap)
	// and, for a String field, calls `__str_decref` — but those live in the SYNTHESIZED body this scan
	// cannot see, so the symbol is recorded and its transitive needs are declared by
	// `noteUnionDestructorUsage` (which has the signatures/interner to classify the fields).
	if bytesStartWith(callee, prefix: DestructorPrefix) 'uniondtor'
		usage.usesHeap = true
		usage.neededDestructors.insert(callee)
	end 'uniondtor'
	// A `<Enum>.fromRawValue(raw)` lookup (`__enum_from_raw_<Enum>`). It declares NOTHING else — the
	// synthesized body compares i64 constants and returns, allocating nothing and calling nothing — which puts
	// it in the `__ucd_` table loads' company as a family whose body this scan need not see past.
	if isEnumLookupRuntimeCallee(callee) 'enumfromraw'
		usage.neededEnumLookups.insert(callee)
	end 'enumfromraw'
	// A String DEEP-CLONE (`__str_clone`, P1.7 slice 3b-vi-b) — a managed array/struct copy cloning a String
	// element/field. It `__mm_alloc`s a fresh fused record and `__str_copy`s the bytes in, inside its SYNTHESIZED
	// body this scan cannot see, so those transitive bits are declared here (the `__str_decref` shape).
	if callee.equals(StrCloneName) 'strclone'
		usage.usesStrClone = true
		usage.usesHeap = true
		usage.usesStrCopy = true
	end 'strclone'
	// The PROVENANCE-DECIDING co-ownership of a borrowed byte record (`__str_retain`, W56). Its body calls
	// BOTH of its two arms' entry points inside a Std body this scan cannot see, so both are declared here —
	// `__str_clone` with the two bits that clone itself declares, and `__mm_incref` with `usesIncref`. Stated
	// directly rather than by re-entering the `strclone` arm above: a transitive need is a fact about THIS
	// entry point, and routing it through another arm's name would make the two families' bits move together
	// the day either changes.
	if callee.equals(StrRetainName) 'strretain'
		usage.usesStrRetain = true
		usage.usesStrClone = true
		usage.usesStrCopy = true
		usage.usesIncref = true
		usage.usesHeap = true
	end 'strretain'
	// The `General_Category` family (P1.8 Slice D; the two raw table loads added at W64, the last of the three
	// entry points after W129 deleted `__ucd_cat`). It declares NOTHING else — see `usesUcdCategory` — which
	// makes it the second family, beside `__clock_*`, whose bodies call nothing this scan cannot see. A PREFIX,
	// exactly as `__clock_*` and `__gt_*` are: both entry points read the same two tables and carry the same
	// nothing else, so "does this program touch the UCD tables?" is one question with one answer.
	if isUcdRuntimeCallee(callee) 'ucdtables'
		usage.usesUcdCategory = true
	end 'ucdtables'
	// ⚠ **THE ORDER IS A RULE ABOUT FAMILIES, NOT ABOUT TODAY'S ARMS, WHICH IS WHY IT SURVIVES EVERY ARM THAT
	// FORCED IT.** A family that closes into another must record FIRST: a bit turned on after its own family's
	// closure has run installs an entry point without what it calls. Two families have stood ahead of these
	// for exactly that reason and both are gone — a `StringIndex` one (W49 wave 4, with `String.slice`) and a
	// `CharacterSet` one (W129, with the `__cs_*` runtime, whose probe decoded a codepoint).
	recordStringMethodUsage(callee, usage: usage)
	recordGraphemeUsage(callee, usage: usage)
	// The three MANAGED-element array copies (`__managed_slice_managed`/`__managed_clone_managed`/`__managed_append_managed`,
	// P1.7 slice 3b-vi-b). `isManagedMemoryRuntimeCallee` above already set `usesManagedMemory`/`usesHeap` (they are `__managed_`-
	// prefixed); THIS bit gates their install so a trivial-array program does not carry them.
	if isManagedElementCopyCallee(callee) 'managedcopy'
		usage.usesManagedElementCopy = true
	end 'managedcopy'
	// The OPAQUE-`T` array copies (`__managed_slice_opaque`/`__managed_append_opaque`, P1.7 slice 3b-vi-b-β).
	// `isManagedMemoryRuntimeCallee` above already set `usesManagedMemory`/`usesHeap` (they are `__managed_`-
	// prefixed); THIS turns on the opaque-copy install AND `usesManagedElementCopy`, because each wrapper's managed
	// branch calls the α `__managed_*_managed` helpers whose own install is gated on that bit.
	if isOpaqueElementCopyCallee(callee) 'opaquecopy'
		usage.usesOpaqueElementCopy = true
		usage.usesManagedElementCopy = true
	end 'opaquecopy'
	// A per-type CLONER (`__clone_<T>`, P1.7 slice 3b-vi-b) — the CLONE twin of the `__destruct_<T>` arm above,
	// referenced through a `funcAddr(__clone_<T>)` element-cloner stamp. It frees no memory but ALLOCATES a box
	// (`usesHeap`), and its transitive `__str_clone`/`__managed_clone`/nested-cloner needs close through
	// `noteClonerUsage` (which has the signatures/interner to classify the fields).
	if bytesStartWith(callee, prefix: ClonerPrefix) 'structcloner'
		usage.usesHeap = true
		usage.neededCloners.insert(callee)
	end 'structcloner'
end 'recordCallUsage'

// ⚠⚠ **`recordCharacterSetUsage` AND `closeCharacterSetNeeds` STOOD HERE AND WENT WITH THE `__cs_*` RUNTIME
// (W129).** The pair is worth one paragraph because what it declared is the obligation any future synthesized
// family inherits, and it is invisible from the corpus: a closure declares what a SYNTHESIZED body calls that
// this Maxon-module scan cannot see (`__cs_contains` decoded a codepoint and probed a `Set with Character`),
// and a family that closes into another must RECORD ahead of it. `recordStringMethodUsage`/`closeStringNeeds`
// and `recordGraphemeUsage`/`closeGraphemeNeeds` below are the same pair for the families that remain.

// ⭐ The P1.8 Slice B grapheme family's arm of the callee → usage-bit map — the `recordStringMethodUsage`
// twin, split out for the same reason: `recordCallUsage` stays a readable list of families. Direct
// references only; what each referenced body in turn needs is closed by `closeGraphemeNeeds`.
function recordGraphemeUsage(callee ByteArray, usage RuntimeUsage)
	if callee.equals(CharAtName) 'charat'
		usage.usesCharAt = true
	end 'charat'
	if callee.equals(CharFromCodepointName) 'charfromcp'
		usage.usesCharFromCodepoint = true
	end 'charfromcp'
	if callee.equals(CharCmpName) 'charcmp'
		usage.usesCharCmp = true
	end 'charcmp'
	if callee.equals(CharAsciiValueName) 'charascii'
		usage.usesCharAsciiValue = true
	end 'charascii'
	if callee.equals(StrToBytesName) 'tobytes'
		usage.usesStrToBytes = true
	end 'tobytes'
	closeGraphemeNeeds(usage)
end 'recordGraphemeUsage'

// ⭐ **WHAT EACH GRAPHEME BODY CALLS THAT THIS SCAN CANNOT SEE.** The family's members call each other from
// inside their SYNTHESIZED Std bodies, which walk no Maxon module — so the dependency is DECLARED here
// rather than discovered, exactly as `closeStringMethodNeeds` declares the String family's. Run as a
// CLOSURE after every record (rather than each arm listing its own transitive set) so the answer cannot
// depend on the order in which the module happens to name its entry points.
// ⚠ **THE SEGMENTER'S OWN NEEDS ARE NO LONGER CLOSED HERE, BECAUSE IT IS NO LONGER SYNTHESIZED.** This
// closure used to walk `__gr_count` → `__gr_end` → `__utf8_cp_at` / `__gr_break` → `__ext_pict` / `__gbp`,
// which is exactly the chain now living in `stdlib/helpers/string/grapheme.maxon`. Those are ORDINARY Maxon
// call edges, so the query spine follows them without being told: what a corpus function needs is found, not
// declared. What is left here is the residue with no corpus counterpart — the `Character` mint and the three
// array-filling views.
// ⚠ **AND THE EDGE *FROM* THESE Std BODIES *INTO* THE CORPUS IS NO LONGER ONE OF THEM.** It was the one thing
// a scan of the Maxon module could not see, declared by `StdlibSource.markCorpusCalleesOfSynthesizedRuntime`;
// every body that had such an edge has retired (the last, `__cs_contains`, at W129) and that seed is deleted.
// A body added here that CALLS a corpus function brings it back — this closure declares runtime-usage BITS
// and cannot declare a stdlib function's REACHABILITY, which is why the two were never one place.
function closeGraphemeNeeds(usage RuntimeUsage)
	// `__char_at` builds a fresh owned inline record: one `__mm_alloc` plus a `__str_copy` blit.
	if usage.usesCharAt 'mintsCharacters'
		usage.usesHeap = true
		usage.usesStrCopy = true
	end 'mintsCharacters'
	// `__char_from_cp` builds the same record with one `__mm_alloc` and NO blit — it stores the bytes it
	// encoded, so it needs the allocator and not the copier.
	if usage.usesCharFromCodepoint 'encodesCharacters'
		usage.usesHeap = true
	end 'encodesCharacters'
	// `__str_to_bytes` pushes into the caller's array.
	//
	// ⚠ **A `pushesDecodedUnits` CLAUSE STOOD BESIDE IT FOR `__str_to_codepoints`/`__str_to_utf16` AND WENT
	// WITH THEM (W49 wave 6).** It declared the same two bits and, in prose, the edge INTO the corpus
	// decoders that a scan of this module cannot see — an edge `StdlibSource` owns because what it turns on is
	// a stdlib function's REACHABILITY rather than a runtime-usage bit. The corpus's own
	// `CodepointView`/`UTF16View` reach `utf8DecodeAt`/`utf8ByteLengthAt` through ordinary Maxon call edges,
	// which the query spine follows without being told.
	if usage.usesStrToBytes 'pushesBytes'
		usage.usesManagedMemory = true
		usage.usesHeap = true
	end 'pushesBytes'
end 'closeGraphemeNeeds'

// The String-building and byte-viewing entry points' own arm of the callee → usage-bit map, split out so
// `recordCallUsage` stays a readable list of families rather than growing four more inline arms. Direct
// references only; what each referenced body in turn needs is closed by `closeStringMethodNeeds`. (It was
// NINE arms until the P1.8 Slice C family retired onto the corpus, and SIX until wave 7 took the two byte
// READS with `byteAt`/`byteAtOrPanic` — see the grave at the head of this file.)
function recordStringMethodUsage(callee ByteArray, usage RuntimeUsage)
	if callee.equals(StrFromBytesName) 'frombytes'
		usage.usesStrFromBytes = true
	end 'frombytes'
	if callee.equals(StrOfBufferName) 'ofbuffer'
		usage.usesStrOfBuffer = true
	end 'ofbuffer'
	if callee.equals(MmToCStringName) 'tocstring'
		usage.usesMmToCString = true
	end 'tocstring'
	if callee.equals(StrBytesViewName) 'bytesview'
		usage.usesStrBytesView = true
	end 'bytesview'
	closeStringMethodNeeds(usage)
end 'recordStringMethodUsage'

// ⭐ **WHAT EACH REFERENCED BODY CALLS THAT THIS SCAN CANNOT SEE.** These entry points call the heap
// helpers from inside their SYNTHESIZED Std bodies, which walk no Maxon module — so the dependency is
// DECLARED here rather than discovered, exactly as `__str_decref` declares its `__mm_alloc`/`__mm_free`.
// Run as a CLOSURE after every record (rather than each arm listing its own transitive set) so the answer
// cannot depend on the order in which the module happens to name its entry points.
function closeStringMethodNeeds(usage RuntimeUsage)
	// Every string-BUILDING entry point allocates a fused record and blits bytes into it
	// (`emitBuildOwnedInlineString` ⇒ `__mm_alloc` + `__str_copy`).
	//
	// ⚠ **A RETIREMENT SHEDS A TERM; ONLY THE LAST TERM SHEDS THE CLAUSE — AND THIS FILE HAS NOW DONE BOTH.**
	// `usesStrMapCase`/`usesStrReplace`/`usesStrReplaceFirst` left this clause as TERMS at W49 wave 2 and
	// `usesStrSplit` at wave 5, each time leaving the surviving terms to keep it alive. Two SIBLING clauses
	// did not survive wave 5, because `split` was their only term: `clonesReceiver` (`split` answered a
	// degenerate empty delimiter with `__str_clone` of the receiver) and `pushesSegments` (it pushed into the
	// array the parser created for it), plus the `needsFind`/`needsRangeEq` pair that installed the search
	// underneath. Deleting a clause while any term could still be true is what would leave a callee
	// uninstalled and a call landing on nothing; deleting it only when nothing can set its last term is what
	// keeps this function from listing conditions no program can meet. What the retired members need instead
	// is declared where a corpus body's needs always are — by the ordinary reachability of what it calls.
	if usage.usesStrFromBytes or usage.usesStrOfBuffer or usage.usesMmToCString 'buildsStrings'
		usage.usesHeap = true
		usage.usesStrCopy = true
	end 'buildsStrings'
	// `__str_bytes_view` increfs the receiver's own record through `__mm_incref` inside its synthesized body,
	// which this scan cannot see — and the value it hands back is a buffer the CALLER drops through
	// `__managed_decref`. `usesManagedMemory` is what installs the managed-memory runtime AND (through
	// `installMmRuntime`'s `usesIncref or usesManagedMemory`) the incref helper, so the two bits below are the
	// whole of its transitive floor.
	//
	// ⚠ **IT PUBLISHED A FRESH `Array` RECORD THROUGH `__managed_create` UNTIL W157**, which is where this
	// clause's `usesManagedMemory` originally came from. The bit is still owed — `__managed_decref` is in that
	// runtime — so what changed is the REASON and not the answer, which is exactly why it is written down.
	if usage.usesStrBytesView 'publishesAView'
		usage.usesManagedMemory = true
		usage.usesHeap = true
	end 'publishesAView'
end 'closeStringMethodNeeds'
